======================================================================================================================
k_cross-scale_mwmass -- CANDIDATE 3: the Milky Way's baryonic mass from a wide-binary velocity ratio
      M_b = e(gamma_v^2) a_0 R_0^2 / G,   B(e) = nu(e)[1+L(e)/3],   a_0 = (c/2) sqrt(G rho_DE)
======================================================================================================================

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1.  IS THE LAW INVERTIBLE?  B(e) must be strictly monotone over the range the band occupies.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  [PASS] B(e) is strictly DECREASING over e = 1e-3 to 30, hence invertible on the branch where B > 1  [CAN FAIL]   (B(1e-3) = 26.855 -> B(30) = 1.00035, 0 non-decreasing steps)
  [PASS] the boost DIPS BELOW 1 at very strong external field -- B - 1 ~ e^{-sqrt e}(1 - sqrt(e)/6) changes sign at e = 36 -- so 'B > 1 always' would have been wrong  [CAN FAIL]   (B crosses 1 downward at e = 35.8 (analytic sqrt(e) = 6, e = 36); B(100) = 0.999970)
  [PASS] the inversion round-trips e -> B -> e to 1e-6  [CAN FAIL]   (max relative error 2.89e-15)
  B is UNBOUNDED as e -> 0: B(1e-3) = 26.86, B(1e-6) = 833.8  (it goes as (5/6)/sqrt(e)).
  So the estimator has a solution for ANY gamma_v > 1 -- there is no upper ceiling.  Its restriction is the
  other way: gamma_v <= 1 has no solution, which is exactly the LambdaCDM/Newtonian prediction.
  [PASS] the estimator is defined precisely on gamma_v > 1 and undefined on gamma_v <= 1, so the Newtonian alternative sits exactly on its boundary  [CAN FAIL]   (B(1e-6) = 833.8 (no ceiling); e(B = 1) = inf)

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2.  THE CIRCULARITY AUDIT.  This check is BUILT TO FIRE.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  the frozen registration adopts a TRUE local field x_ext = 1.89929 a_0, i.e. e = 1.28903, nu(e) = 1.47342
    [canonical] a_0 = 9.360e-11: the registration's OWN assumption is M_b = 5.709e+10 Msun; B(e_reg) = 1.34143; inverting that B returns M_b = 5.709e+10 Msun
    [alt      ] a_0 = 1.130e-10: the registration's OWN assumption is M_b = 6.892e+10 Msun; B(e_reg) = 1.34143; inverting that B returns M_b = 6.892e+10 Msun
  [PASS] CIRCULARITY CONFIRMED: feeding the registration's own assumed field through B and back returns the SAME mass to 0.1%, so quoting this inversion TODAY measures the assumption, not the Galaxy  [CAN FAIL]   (max round-trip departure 4.44e-16 -- the estimator is exactly circular until gamma_v is MEASURED)

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3.  BUG PATTERN 2 -- a SPHERICAL formula applied to a DISC.  How big is the error?
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  g_N,ext = G M_b/R_0^2 treats the Galaxy as a point.  The exact razor-thin exponential-disc field is
  g_R = 4 pi G Sigma_0 R_d y^2 [I0K0 - I1K1](y), y = R/(2R_d).  Compare at R_0 = 8.122 kpc:
     pure exponential disc, R_d = 2.15 kpc:  g_disc/g_point = 1.1761   (inferred M_b moves by x0.850)
     pure exponential disc, R_d = 2.50 kpc:  g_disc/g_point = 1.1195   (inferred M_b moves by x0.893)
     pure exponential disc, R_d = 2.60 kpc:  g_disc/g_point = 1.0996   (inferred M_b moves by x0.909)
     pure exponential disc, R_d = 3.00 kpc:  g_disc/g_point = 1.0111   (inferred M_b moves by x0.989)
     McMillan-2017-like COMPOSITE (bulge 0.90 + thin 3.52 [Rd 2.5] + thick 1.06 [Rd 3.0] + gas 1.20 [Rd 7.0], total 6.680e+10 Msun):
        g_N(R_0) = 1.3398e-10 m/s^2 = 1.4314 a_0 (canonical) / 1.1856 a_0 (alt);  g_composite/g_point = 0.9490
  [PASS] the point-mass approximation is NOT innocuous at R_0: the geometry factor departs from 1 by more than 5%  [CAN FAIL]   (g/g_point = 1.1761 (pure disc, R_d = 2.15 kpc) down to 0.9490; the composite model gives 0.9490, i.e. a 5% upward correction to every mass this estimator returns)
  AGAINST INTEREST: a real Galaxy has a bulge, which pushes g back UP toward the point-mass value, so the
  honest statement is a systematic BAND on the inferred mass, x0.85 to x1.05, carried as GEOM below.
  SEPARATE FINDING, against interest: the composite baryon census's own Newtonian field at R_0 is
     e = g_N/a_0 = 1.4314 (canonical), whereas the frozen registration adopts e = 1.2890, 11% lower.

